    {
      "title": "An empirical study of retrieval-augmented diffusion language models for generative commonsense reasoning",
      "authors": [
        "Yubo Fang",
        "Hai-Tao Yu",
        "Hideo Joho",
        "Sumio Fujita",
        "Yan Ge",
        "Chao Lei"
      ],
      "abstract": "Generative commonsense reasoning (GCR) remains challenging for intelligent systems, as large language models (LLMs) rely primarily on parametric knowledge and often lack sufficient commonsense information. Although retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) alleviates this limitation by incorporating external knowledge, it has been studied primarily with autoregressive language models, motivating an examination of retrieval augmentation in diffusion-based generation. In this work, we present GRADi4GCR 1 as a systematic empirical study of retrieval-augmented diffusion generation for GCR. GRADi4GCR defines a common retrieval-augmented conditional-generation formulation that retrieves commonsense knowledge from an external corpus and incorporates it throughout diffusion-based generation. By instantiating this formulation with continuous, latent, and discrete diffusion language model (DLM) backbones, GRADi4GCR enables within-backbone evaluation of retrieval augmentation and characterizes knowledge integration across the three DLM paradigms. Experiments on two GCR benchmarks show consistent gains from retrieval augmentation across all evaluated DLM backbones under matched within-backbone comparisons, while absolute cross-model scores are reported descriptively. Additional analyses examine generation diversity and fluency, retrieval strategies, and qualitative generation behavior. These findings motivate further investigation of DLMs as an alternative paradigm for retrieval-augmented commonsense reasoning.",
